# twoody-server runtime env.
#
# Suggested install path: /etc/twoody-server.env (chmod 0600, owned by
# root). Referenced by `scripts/twoody-server.service`.
#
# Fill in the values that apply to your deployment; leave the rest
# blank. Comments below explain which subsystem each block unlocks.

# ── Network ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PORT=3000
HOST=0.0.0.0
LOG_LEVEL=info

# ── Where this server keeps its data ─────────────────────────────────
# The database, the setup token and the session keys. Defaults to
# ~/.twoody/data of the user the service runs as. Set it explicitly if
# that user's home is not where you want them; the systemd unit's
# ReadWritePaths has to name the same place.
# TWOODY_DATA_DIR=/home/pi/.twoody/data

# A name for this machine, shown in the apps that pair with it.
# TWOODY_INSTANCE_NAME=twoody-pi

# ── Nothing leaves this machine ──────────────────────────────────────
# Refuses every outgoing call to a host that is not on this machine or
# this private network — including the model APIs, the upstream bridge
# below, and the relay. Set it when this server is meant to answer with
# what it has and nothing else.
# TWOODY_LOCAL_ONLY=1

# ── Administration page ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# /admin answers only to this machine unless a token is set. Set one to
# open it from another device on the network; 16 characters minimum,
# and it is the whole of the protection.
# TWOODY_ADMIN_TOKEN=

# ── Names and web pages ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# The server answers only to the names it is known by: any IP address,
# localhost, names of the local network (a single word such as a Docker
# service name, or ending in .local, .lan, .home, .home.arpa, .internal),
# this machine's own name and the host of TWOODY_PUBLIC_URL. Anything
# else is refused (421), which keeps a web page from reaching it by
# pointing its own domain at this machine. Behind a reverse proxy, or
# through a name such as pi.example.ts.net, list it here (comma-separated;
# ".example.com" allows every name under it, "*" turns the check off).
# TWOODY_ALLOWED_HOSTS=twoody.example.com
# Web pages allowed to read its answers, besides Twoody Desktop's window
# and pages served from this machine (http://localhost:…), exactly as the
# browser sends them in Origin.
# TWOODY_CORS_ORIGINS=https://twoody.example.com

# ── First account ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Normally the server prints a setup token at first boot and you type
# it into the app. Set these to choose them yourself instead — useful
# when the console is not somewhere you can read.
# TWOODY_SETUP_TOKEN=
# TWOODY_PAIRING_CODE=

# ── Reaching this server from outside the network ────────────────────
# Off by default. With an account configured below, the server waits at
# the relay for the devices paired with it on this network, so a phone
# away from home reaches it without a port being opened here. What
# travels is sealed between the two devices; the relay carries bytes it
# cannot read. Refused outright in local-only mode.
# TWOODY_TUNNEL=1

# ── Twoody account ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `twoody-server login` adds this server to your Twoody account: it
# prints a code to enter on a device signed in to it (twoody.com/appareil,
# Twoody Desktop or the app), and keeps what it gets in account.json in
# the data directory (mode 0600). Then it sends its status every 30 s,
# answers the account's devices it is allowed to (the device that added
# it, one another device vouched for, or `twoody-server approve` here),
# lends its models (« Partager ») and waits at the relay.
#
# Its name in the account (default: the host name).
# TWOODY_COMPUTER_NAME=Raspberry Pi du salon
# Its id in the account, for a server tied to it by TWOODY_USER_TOKEN
# rather than by `twoody-server login` (which knows it).
# TWOODY_COMPUTER_ID=
# The address the account's devices reach it at on this network, handed
# to them when they are allowed (default: its first private IPv4).
# TWOODY_PUBLIC_URL=http://192.168.1.42:3000
# Set to 0 to keep, once linked, the bridge client or the tunnel off.
# TWOODY_ACCOUNT_BRIDGE=0
# TWOODY_ACCOUNT_TUNNEL=0
# What another computer of the account may send it: chat, background
# (memory extraction), both (default), or nothing (empty).
# TWOODY_PEER_ACCEPTS=chat,background
# Set to 0 when its models cannot be called with tools.
# TWOODY_PEER_TOOLS=0
# Its load (CPU, memory, GPU) is sampled every 15 s for the usage screen
# and the router; 0 turns that off.
# TWOODY_MACHINE_SAMPLER=0

# ── In-process bridge executor ───────────────────────────────────────
# Set to 1 on headless Linux deployments where no external Mac Bridge
# worker will long-poll /bridge/tasks/next. Runs `search`, `read`,
# `list_folders`, `list_dir`, and `llm_infer` in the same Node
# process. Off by default for backward compatibility with installs
# that still pair with a Mac.
TWOODY_SELF_BRIDGE=1

# Folders the bridge is allowed to search/read. Accepts:
#   /path1:/path2              (colon-separated)
#   /path1,/path2              (comma-separated)
#   ["/path1","/path2"]        (JSON array)
# Optional `Name=` prefix gives a folder a friendly label:
#   Documents=/home/pi/Documents:Media=/srv/share
TWOODY_BRIDGE_FOLDERS=Documents=/home/pi/Documents

# ── Model engine (« Moteur ») ────────────────────────────────────────
# Which engine answers on this computer. By default the choice is kept in
# runtime.json in the data directory, set with
#   twoody-server config set runtime=builtin      (or ollama, lmstudio, mlx, auto, an http(s) address)
# and from the apps. « builtin » is llama.cpp run by this server itself:
#   twoody-server runtime install                 (the pinned llama.cpp, checked against its SHA-256)
#   twoody-server models add Qwen3.5-4B-Q4_K_M    (twoody-server models list)
# started on the first question and stopped after idle_minutes (30) of
# silence. « auto » takes the first engine with a model: built-in, then
# Ollama, LM Studio, mlx-lm. An NVIDIA GPU: use Ollama (runtime=ollama).
# TWOODY_RUNTIME forces the engine whatever runtime.json says.
# TWOODY_RUNTIME=builtin
# Where Ollama is, when it is not on this computer's port 11434.
# TWOODY_OLLAMA_URL=http://127.0.0.1:11434
# Where the built-in runtime and its models live (default: runtime/ and
# models/ in the data directory). The systemd unit's ReadWritePaths has
# to name them when they are moved elsewhere.
# TWOODY_RUNTIME_DIR=/home/pi/.twoody/data/runtime
# TWOODY_MODELS_DIR=/home/pi/.twoody/data/models
# A catalogue of models other than Twoody's (JSON), for tests.
# TWOODY_MODEL_CATALOG=

# ── Another local runtime (Ollama / mlx-lm / vLLM / …) ───────────────
# OpenAI-compatible /v1 root, used with no runtime.json yet (a server set
# up before the built-in engine keeps answering with it: 11434 becomes
# runtime=ollama, any other address runtime=<that address>). Ollama
# listens on 11434 by default; mlx-lm on 8080. Leave it out for the
# built-in engine.
# BRIDGE_LOCAL_LLM_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1

# ── Home Assistant integration ───────────────────────────────────────
# When set, the `smart_home` tool calls these endpoints. Token is a
# long-lived access token created in HA → Profile → Long-lived tokens.
# HOME_ASSISTANT_URL=http://192.168.1.89:8123
# HOME_ASSISTANT_TOKEN=...

# ── Upstream bridge client ───────────────────────────────────────────
# When TWOODY_BACKEND_URL + TWOODY_USER_TOKEN are set, this server
# also long-polls an upstream Twoody backend (the managed cloud at
# api.twoody.com, or a self-hosted Rails) and claims smart_home_proxy
# / search / read / llm_infer / list_* tasks routed to the user's LAN.
# Used by mobile clients that talk to api.twoody.com but need a
# LAN-side worker for local-only resources (Home Assistant, files,
# private LLM). Leave blank when this server is only used directly by
# LAN clients (CLI/Desktop pointing at this host).
# TWOODY_BACKEND_URL=https://api.twoody.com
# TWOODY_USER_TOKEN=...
# TWOODY_BRIDGE_DEVICE_NICKNAME=twoody-pi
#
# Local models lent to that backend (llm_infer). Several runtimes may be
# listed; a model goes to the one that holds it. The default model is
# what runs when an app asks for "the local model" without naming one.
# BRIDGE_LOCAL_LLM_ENDPOINTS=http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1,http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1
# BRIDGE_LOCAL_LLM_MODEL=qwen3:8b

# ── Bonjour / mDNS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Advertise this server as `_twoody._tcp` on the LAN so the mobile and
# desktop apps can discover it without typing an IP. Set to 1 to opt
# out (CI, Docker bridge networks).
# TWOODY_BONJOUR_DISABLED=

# ── Spotify Web API ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# When all three are set, the local `spotify` tool (play / pause /
# next / previous / volume / search_and_play) actually drives the
# user's Spotify account. Obtain via the Authorization Code flow once
# (one-time browser dance on developer.spotify.com). SPOTIFY_DEVICE_ID
# is optional — Spotify Connect device the playback target pins to;
# omit to use the "active" device on the account.
# SPOTIFY_CLIENT_ID=
# SPOTIFY_CLIENT_SECRET=
# SPOTIFY_REFRESH_TOKEN=
# SPOTIFY_DEVICE_ID=

# ── Local STT (whisper.cpp) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Paths to the whisper-cli binary + a ggml model. Advertised as
# `audio-transcribe-provider` when both files exist; missing →
# /audio/transcriptions and the `transcribe` bridge kind both return
# 503 `whisper_not_configured`.
# WHISPER_BIN=/home/pi/whisper.cpp/build/bin/whisper-cli
# WHISPER_MODEL=/home/pi/whisper.cpp/models/ggml-base.bin
# WHISPER_THREADS=4

# ── Scanned documents (OCR) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# A PDF page without text of its own (a scan) is read, for the document
# index and the chat, by this helper: Twoody for Mac points it at its
# bundled twoody-ocr (Apple's Vision framework, on the Mac, no network).
# Unset, scans stay unread. Languages in order of preference.
# TWOODY_OCR_BIN=/Applications/Twoody Desktop.app/Contents/Resources/ocr/twoody-ocr
# TWOODY_OCR_LANGUAGES=fr-FR,en-US

# ── Local OCR (tesseract) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Tesseract binary + default language. Advertised as `ocr-provider`
# when the binary exists. Install with:
#   apt install tesseract-ocr tesseract-ocr-fra tesseract-ocr-eng
# TESSERACT_BIN=/usr/bin/tesseract
# TESSERACT_LANG=fra+eng

# Optional local semantic document search (off until enabled in Documents).
# Desktop supplies these paths. Standalone servers need the pinned Qwen3
# Embedding 0.6B Q8_0 model and a compatible llama-server binary.
# TWOODY_EMBEDDING_BINARY=/absolute/path/to/llama-server
# TWOODY_EMBEDDING_MODEL=/absolute/path/to/Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B-Q8_0.gguf
